00:00:08.017 --> 00:00:09.600
ENO COMPTON: If
you're a Go developer,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.540
you're probably
excited about Go 1.11.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:13.820
But you might also be
wondering, does App Engine

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.260
support the new runtime?

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.254
Can I write idiomatic Go
and run it on App Engine?

00:00:18.254 --> 00:00:20.420
ANGELA FUNK: And if you're
writing Go for the cloud,

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.270
the last thing you want to
do is worry about maintaining

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:25.220
and deploying infrastructure.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.590
App Engine, Google's
platform as a service,

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.280
handles all that for you.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.880
So you can focus on what's
important-- writing Go code.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:33.714
Let's show them how.

00:00:33.714 --> 00:00:35.213
ENO COMPTON: We're
going to show you

00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:37.820
how to deploy a Go application
to App Engine using the new Go

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.480
1.11 runtime.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.720
ANGELA FUNK: Now, with
the new Go 1.11 runtime,

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:44.600
it's incredibly easy to
write idiomatic Go code

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.100
and deploy it to App Engine.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.170
All you need are three things--

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.560
your Go code, a YAML file
that describes your runtime,

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:54.240
and the vendor directory
with your dependencies.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.040
Let's take a look at each one.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:58.490
ENO COMPTON: First, we'll
write a small HTTP server that

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:00.980
listens on port 8080 and
responds with "hello world"

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.240
to all requests.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:04.849
Our code is idiomatic
and totally unconcerned

00:01:04.849 --> 00:01:06.649
with the underlying
infrastructure.

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:08.690
ANGELA FUNK: Next, we need
to specify the runtime

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:09.960
we want for our app.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.320
So in the root of our
project, add an app YAML

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.340
file with just one line in it--

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.105
go111.

00:01:16.105 --> 00:01:18.230
ENO COMPTON: And finally,
here's our file structure

00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:19.690
with a vendor directory.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:21.230
We're using Go
support for rendering

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:23.840
our dependencies, which the
new runtime supports out

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.040
of the box.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.900
Also, if you're
interested in trying out

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.002
Go support for modules, the
runtime supports that as well.

00:01:30.002 --> 00:01:32.210
ANGELA FUNK: Now that we've
finished writing our app,

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:33.230
let's deploy it.

00:01:33.230 --> 00:01:34.900
It takes only one command--

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.090
gcloud app deploy.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.149
Let's start a timer.

00:01:40.149 --> 00:01:42.440
ENO COMPTON: While we're
waiting for the app to deploy,

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.939
let's talk about what makes App
Engine a great choice for Go

00:01:44.939 --> 00:01:46.220
applications.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:48.350
ANGELA FUNK: First,
the new Go runtime.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:51.170
Not only does it have first
class support for vendoring,

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.120
it also supports Go modules.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.340
You can use any
library framework

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.290
that makes sense for
your application,

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.710
all while writing that
familiar idiomatic Go

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:01.420
code that all Gopher's love.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.920
ENO COMPTON: And you get the
benefits of Google App Engine's

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:04.910
standard environment.

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:08.090
Things like fast deployments,
building in the cloud,

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:11.090
managed security updates,
support for HTTPS,

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:12.710
scaling to zero when
the app is idle,

00:02:12.710 --> 00:02:15.650
in addition to logging
metrics and error reporting.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.690
ANGELA FUNK: And with
that, our app is deployed.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:18.299
There it is.

00:02:18.299 --> 00:02:20.840
ENO COMPTON: We're both really
excited about the new runtime,

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.090
and we hope you are, too.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:23.006
I'm Eno Compton.

